+  `uninit()` is safe because its result does not claim to contain a `T`. `write` moves `value` into the slot without dropping the previous bytes and returns a mutable reference, with the slot's region, to the newly initialized value. `assume_init` consumes the slot and asserts that it contains a valid initialized `T`; violating that precondition is undefined behavior under the existing unsafe-memory rules. It MUST NOT manufacture a `T` from merely zeroed bytes unless `T: Zeroable`. No safe operation may read the contents as `T` before initialization is established.
+* `[ARN-8a]` **Destruction after initialization.** Moving an initialized `T` out of `MaybeUninit[T]` transfers ownership to the destination and leaves the source slot uninitialized; the source slot runs no destructor. Even when `T: Drop`, `MaybeUninit[T]` itself never runs `T`'s destructor. A destructor runs only after the value has become an ordinary initialized `T` and later reaches an ordinary ownership destruction point.
